1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,420
Speaker 1: Bloomberg's in-house economists just dropped a savage new report that traded their standard China cheerleading for some doom,

2
00:00:08,060 --> 00:00:12,020
Speaker 1: contending that China, quote, may never overtake the U.S. economy.

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,760
Speaker 1: Meanwhile, the U.S. Commerce Secretary is now warning China has become, quote,

4
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:20,960
Speaker 1: uninvestable for foreign companies who had long seen it as a free market paradise.

5
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:27,860
Speaker 1: So this is a big deal because for 35 years now, it has been an article of faith that China was going to inherit the earth.

6
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:32,240
Speaker 1: China's rise was inevitable, and we were all living on borrowed time.

7
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:34,940
Speaker 1: Now, don't get me wrong, we are living on borrowed time.

8
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:40,280
Speaker 1: The disastrous policies made since the 2008 crisis, really since Richard Nixon,

9
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:44,020
Speaker 1: are putting us on an unsustainable path to permanent stagflation,

10
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:50,280
Speaker 1: flattening our growth prospects until we finally hit that financial singularity that is too big to bail out.

11
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:54,720
Speaker 1: The problem is China is headed to the same place, maybe even faster.

12
00:00:54,940 --> 00:00:58,060
Speaker 1: I've done a couple of recent videos on China's rapid collapse.

13
00:00:58,220 --> 00:01:04,560
Speaker 1: In short, Xi channeled trillions to politically favored industries at the expense of the dynamic bits of the economy,

14
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,820
Speaker 1: leading to both manufacturing and property now in free fall.

15
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:12,380
Speaker 1: Those two sectors make up half of China's economy, much more than the U.S.,

16
00:01:12,380 --> 00:01:14,820
Speaker 1: so that's dragging the entire country down,

17
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:19,280
Speaker 1: leading to rising joblessness, especially among the young who always get fired first.

18
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:23,700
Speaker 1: And, as in this country, financial and bank failures spreading like wildfire.

19
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:28,480
Speaker 1: Bigger and bigger they march towards that moment, Beijing can't bail them all out.

20
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,700
Speaker 1: Now, Bloomberg takes this clown show and runs with it, citing deep-seated structural problems,

21
00:01:33,980 --> 00:01:36,740
Speaker 1: predicting a slower China for decades to come.

22
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:42,920
Speaker 1: Specifically, they now think China could momentarily pip the U.S. sometime in the 2040s,

23
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:46,680
Speaker 1: but then immediately fall behind again as China's population shrivels,

24
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:52,020
Speaker 1: thanks to their idiotic one-child policy that appears to have become a social norm.

25
00:01:52,020 --> 00:01:59,080
Speaker 1: So, seven years after one-child ended, China's currently sporting 1.09 births per woman.

26
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:04,340
Speaker 1: For a sense, if that continued for a generation, or for three generations, a century,

27
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:09,200
Speaker 1: China's population would drop eight-fold, to about half what it is in the U.S.

28
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:13,060
Speaker 1: Now, keep in mind, that doesn't mean China's poor, it simply means they're number two,

29
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:15,400
Speaker 1: maybe number three if India keeps plugging away,

30
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:19,200
Speaker 1: which doesn't really matter day-to-day, it's more of a prestige and power thing,

31
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:23,440
Speaker 1: but it is a big change from the doomsday predictions of the past decades,

32
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:28,420
Speaker 1: and it suggests that China may already be past its peak of power.

33
00:02:28,740 --> 00:02:29,620
Speaker 1: So, what's next?

34
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:32,220
Speaker 1: I think Bloomberg's being short-sighted, Xi is not immortal,

35
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:37,620
Speaker 1: and China has a lot more free-market bureaucrats than the U.S., or, God forbid, Europe.

36
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:42,900
Speaker 1: Yes, China's in for some lean days, and those would hit a lot of poor countries like Africa,

37
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:44,860
Speaker 1: who depend on exports to China.

38
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:49,280
Speaker 1: But Xi himself has got a lot of opposition inside the Chinese government,

39
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:54,480
Speaker 1: despite building a personal police state, so he could retire surprisingly soon.

40
00:02:55,080 --> 00:03:00,920
Speaker 1: Moreover, the victory laps are delusional, because unless the West reverses our rapid plunge into socialism,

41
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:05,060
Speaker 1: the Asian policy mix will win, whether or not China's on board.

42
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:12,560
Speaker 1: So, what we see in Korea, Singapore, or Taiwan, where the economy is nurtured because it is the social safety net,

43
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:16,680
Speaker 1: because prosperity leads to the rest, rather than today's West,

44
00:03:16,760 --> 00:03:21,740
Speaker 1: which crushes the real economy to buy votes or to fund activists who prefer to burn things instead.

45
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:23,460
Speaker 1: Okay, we'll be watching.

46
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:24,480
Speaker 1: See you next time.
